**Status:** Not yet fixed - valid issue.

The problem: When we skip enrichment for a merged/closed PR (checking the cache), we return early without copying cached fields to `sessions[i].pr`. The session keeps default `basicPRToDashboard()` values (e.g., `state: "open"`).

**Fix needed in `page.tsx`:**
```typescript
// Skip enrichment if PR is already merged/closed (check cache)
if (core.pr) {
  const cacheKey = prCacheKey(core.pr.owner, core.pr.repo, core.pr.number);
  const cached = prCache.get(cacheKey);
  if (cached && (cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed")) {
    // MISSING: Copy cached data to sessions[i].pr before returning
    if (sessions[i].pr) {
      sessions[i].pr.state = cached.state;
      sessions[i].pr.ciStatus = cached.ciStatus;
      // ... copy all cached fields
    }
    return Promise.resolve();
  }
}
```

Will fix in next commit.

**Fixed** in commit c5bccb7.

Now when we skip enrichment for a merged/closed PR (after checking the cache), we copy all cached fields to `sessions[i].pr` before returning:

```typescript
if (cached && (cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed")) {
  // Apply cached terminal state to session before skipping enrichment
  if (sessions[i].pr) {
    sessions[i].pr.state = cached.state;
    sessions[i].pr.ciStatus = cached.ciStatus;
    sessions[i].pr.reviewDecision = cached.reviewDecision;
    sessions[i].pr.ciChecks = cached.ciChecks;
    sessions[i].pr.mergeability = cached.mergeability;
    sessions[i].pr.pendingComments = cached.pendingComments;
    sessions[i].pr.automatedComments = cached.automatedComments;
  }
  return Promise.resolve();
}
```

Terminal PRs now render with correct cached state instead of stale defaults.

**Fixed** in commit c5bccb7.

Changed from "all requests failed" to "majority failed" detection (>= 50%):

```typescript
// Check if most critical requests failed (likely rate limit)
// Note: Some methods (like getCISummary) return fallback values instead of rejecting,
// so we can't rely on "all rejected" — check if majority failed instead
const failedCount = results.filter((r) => r.status === "rejected").length;
const mostFailed = failedCount >= results.length / 2;

if (mostFailed) {
  console.error(
    `[enrichSessionPR] ${failedCount}/${results.length} API calls failed for PR #${pr.number}:`,
    firstError,
  );
  dashboard.pr.mergeability.blockers = ["API rate limited or unavailable"];
  return;
}
```

Now detects rate limiting even when some SCM methods return fallback/default values (like getCISummary returning "failing" or "none" on error).

**Fixed** in commit 42460c2.

The issue was that terminal status check happened BEFORE cache check, so terminal sessions never received cached data.

**Fix:** Restructured the logic to:
1. Check cache FIRST (before any early returns)
2. Apply cached data if available (for ALL sessions, terminal or not)
3. Skip enrichment for terminal sessions OR merged/closed PRs (data already applied from cache)

**Before:**
```typescript
// Skip enrichment for terminal sessions
if (terminalStatuses.has(core.status)) {
  return Promise.resolve();  // Returns with stale defaults\!
}

// Skip if cached merged/closed (apply cached data)
if (cached && (cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed")) {
  sessions[i].pr = cached;  // Only non-terminal sessions got cached data
  return Promise.resolve();
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Check cache FIRST
const cached = prCache.get(cacheKey);

// Apply cached data if available (terminal or not)
if (cached) {
  sessions[i].pr = cached;  // ALL sessions get cached data

  // Skip enrichment for terminal OR merged/closed
  if (terminalStatuses.has(core.status) || cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed") {
    return Promise.resolve();
  }
}
```

Terminal sessions now show correct cached PR state instead of stale "open" defaults.

**Fixed** in commit 42460c2.

Removed the `!mostFailed` gate on caching - we now always cache enrichment results, including partial data from rate-limited requests.

**Before:**
```typescript
// Add rate-limit blocker if most failed
if (mostFailed) {
  dashboard.pr.mergeability.blockers.push("API rate limited or unavailable");
}

// Cache the result if we got at least some data
if (!mostFailed) {  // ❌ Blocks caching when rate-limited
  prCache.set(cacheKey, cacheData);
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
// Add rate-limit blocker if most failed
if (mostFailed) {
  dashboard.pr.mergeability.blockers.push("API rate limited or unavailable");
}

// Always cache the result (including partial data from rate-limited requests)
// This reduces API pressure during rate-limit periods
prCache.set(cacheKey, cacheData);  // ✅ Always cache
```

**Benefits:**
1. **Reduced API pressure**: During rate-limits, subsequent refreshes use cached partial data instead of re-hitting APIs
2. **Better UX**: Users see cached partial data (even with "API rate limited" blocker) instead of repeated failures
3. **60s TTL protection**: Even if we're caching partial data, it expires after 60s, so we'll retry when the cache expires

Updated test expectations to reflect new behavior - "should cache even when most requests fail (to reduce API pressure)".

**Fixed** in commit e4911a8.

Added missing fields when applying cached data. Now copying ALL cached fields:

**Before** (incomplete):
```typescript
if (cached) {
  sessions[i].pr.state = cached.state;
  // ❌ Missing: title, additions, deletions
  sessions[i].pr.ciStatus = cached.ciStatus;
  sessions[i].pr.reviewDecision = cached.reviewDecision;
  // ...
}
```

**After** (complete):
```typescript
if (cached) {
  sessions[i].pr.state = cached.state;
  sessions[i].pr.title = cached.title;         // ✅ Added
  sessions[i].pr.additions = cached.additions; // ✅ Added
  sessions[i].pr.deletions = cached.deletions; // ✅ Added
  sessions[i].pr.ciStatus = cached.ciStatus;
  sessions[i].pr.reviewDecision = cached.reviewDecision;
  // ...
}
```

Sessions that skip enrichment now display complete cached data including PR title and line change counts.

**Fixed** in commit e4911a8.

Removed the "skip enrichment for terminal with no cache" logic. Terminal sessions now enrich at least once.

**Problem:**
Terminal sessions without cache were permanently skipped, keeping stale default data (pr.state="open") forever after cache expiry or server restart.

**Before:**
```typescript
if (cached) {
  // Apply cached data
  if (terminalStatuses.has(core.status) || cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed") {
    return Promise.resolve(); // Skip with cache
  }
}

// Skip enrichment for terminal sessions with no cache
// ❌ This caused permanent staleness after cache expiry
if (terminalStatuses.has(core.status)) {
  return Promise.resolve(); // Skip without cache → STALE FOREVER
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
if (cached) {
  // Apply cached data
  // Skip enrichment if cache is fresh AND (terminal OR merged/closed)
  if (terminalStatuses.has(core.status) || cached.state === "merged" || cached.state === "closed") {
    return Promise.resolve(); // Skip only when cache exists
  }
}
// ✅ Removed the uncached terminal skip → terminal sessions now enrich once
```

**New behavior:**
1. Terminal session without cache → enrich once → populate cache
2. Terminal session with fresh cache → skip enrichment (use cached data)
3. After 60s cache expiry → enrich again → refresh cache

This ensures terminal sessions get accurate PR data while still avoiding unnecessary API calls when cache is fresh.

✅ Fixed in commit 5a3acf1

Moved the project filter to run **after** `metadataToSession()` so inference happens first.

**The Issue:**
```typescript
// Line 396 - OLD (buggy)
if (projectId && raw["project"] !== projectId) continue;
// ❌ Filters BEFORE inference runs
// Legacy sessions: raw["project"] = undefined → filtered out
```

**The Fix:**
```typescript
// Build session with inference first
const session = metadataToSession(sid, raw, config, createdAt, modifiedAt);

// Line 413 - NEW (correct)
if (projectId && session.projectId !== projectId) continue;
// ✅ Filters AFTER inference runs
// Legacy sessions: session.projectId = "ao" (inferred) → included
```

**Test Results:**
- ✅ `list("ao")` with legacy sessions `ao-1`, `ao-18`
- Before: 0 sessions (filtered out incorrectly)
- After: 2 sessions (correctly included)

**Impact:** Fixes `cleanup(projectId)` for legacy sessions, which delegates to `list(projectId)`.

Fixed in commit 1e520e3.

**Changes:**
1. Replaced duplicated validation logic (lines 613-620) with call to centralized `isRestorable()` helper
2. Removed unused imports: `NON_RESTORABLE_STATUSES`, `TERMINAL_STATUSES`, `TERMINAL_ACTIVITIES`
3. Added import for `isRestorable` function

**Before:**
```typescript
if (NON_RESTORABLE_STATUSES.has(oldStatus)) {
  throw new SessionNotRestorableError(sessionId, oldStatus);
}

if (!TERMINAL_STATUSES.has(oldStatus) && !TERMINAL_ACTIVITIES.has(currentSession.activity)) {
  throw new SessionNotRestorableError(sessionId, oldStatus);
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
if (!isRestorable(currentSession)) {
  throw new SessionNotRestorableError(sessionId, currentSession.status);
}
```

This keeps the session manager consistent with the web API route (which already uses `isRestorable()`) and ensures validation logic stays in sync.

Fixed in commit 096cf3c.

**Problem:** The endpoint returned HTTP 400 (Bad Request) for `SessionNotRestorableError`, but 400 implies the request is malformed. The error actually indicates a state conflict — the request is well-formed but the session's current state prevents restoration.

**Solution:** Return HTTP 409 (Conflict) instead:
```typescript
if (error instanceof SessionNotRestorableError) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: error.message }, { status: 409 });
}
```

This correctly indicates a resource state conflict and restores the previous API contract that tests expected.

Fixed in commit 096cf3c.

**Problem:** The route did pre-validation with `get()` and `isRestorable()` before calling `restore()`, which internally does the exact same checks. This caused:
- 2 `get()` calls (2 disk reads + 2 subprocess checks for runtime liveness)
- Redundant validation logic duplication
- Only the 404 check provided unique value

**Solution:** Remove pre-validation entirely and let `restore()` handle all validation:
```typescript
try {
  const session = await sessionManager.restore(id);
  return NextResponse.json({ ok: true, session: sessionToDashboard(session) });
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof SessionNotRestorableError) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: error.message }, { status: 409 });
  }
  if (error.message.includes("not found")) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: error.message }, { status: 404 });
  }
  // ...
}
```

Now only one `get()` call happens (inside `restore()`), reducing I/O overhead by 50%.

Fixed in commit 842c810.

**Problem:** `getRestoreCommand` handled the `permissions` flag but omitted the `--model` flag. For projects configured with non-default models (e.g., `agentConfig.model: "opus"`), restored sessions would silently use the default model instead of the configured one.

**Solution:** Add `--model` flag handling, mirroring `getLaunchCommand` behavior:

1. **In getRestoreCommand** (agent-claude-code plugin):
```typescript
// Check model: prefer metadata (new sessions), fall back to project config (old sessions)
const model = session.metadata?.["model"] ?? project.agentConfig?.model;
if (model) {
  parts.push("--model", shellEscape(model));
}
```

2. **Write model to metadata during spawn** (session-manager):
```typescript
writeMetadata(config.dataDir, sessionId, {
  // ...
  permissions: project.agentConfig?.permissions,
  model: project.agentConfig?.model,  // Added
});
```

3. **Add model field to SessionMetadata type**

Now restored sessions preserve their original model configuration, whether from metadata (new sessions) or project config (old sessions pre-dating this PR).

✅ Fixed in commit 55ab63d

Added runtime validation that throws immediately if `project` field is missing.

**The Issue:**
While TypeScript types enforce `project` as required, there was no runtime check. If metadata somehow lacked the field (corruption, legacy script, etc.), it would be `undefined` at runtime, causing the original bug.

**The Fix:**
```typescript
function metadataToSession(sessionId, meta, ...) {
  // Runtime validation: project field is required
  if (!meta["project"]) {
    throw new Error(
      `Session ${sessionId} metadata is missing required 'project' field. ` +
      `Add 'project=<project-id>' to the metadata file.`
    );
  }

  return {
    projectId: meta["project"],  // ✓ Guaranteed to exist now
    // ...
  };
}
```

**Why This Is Safe:**
1. ✅ All 22 existing sessions manually fixed with `project=ao`
2. ✅ TypeScript code writes `project` field for new sessions (line 354)
3. ✅ Now runtime validation catches any edge cases
4. ✅ Fails fast with clear error message (not silently undefined)

**Result:** Both compile-time AND runtime enforcement of required field.

Fixed — now using `TextDecoder` to properly reconstruct UTF-8 from the base64 payload:

```typescript
const binary = atob(b64);
const bytes = Uint8Array.from(binary, (c) => c.charCodeAt(0));
const text = new TextDecoder().decode(bytes);
navigator.clipboard?.writeText(text).catch(() => {});
```

This correctly handles non-ASCII characters (accented letters, emoji, CJK, etc.).

Fixed. The `expectedHeight < 700` heuristic was fundamentally broken on large screens — replaced it with frame-to-frame height stabilisation:

```typescript
const currentHeight = container.getBoundingClientRect().height;
const settled = lastHeight >= 0 && Math.abs(currentHeight - lastHeight) < 1;
lastHeight = currentHeight;
```

The loop now exits as soon as the container height stops changing (within 1px tolerance), typically after just 2 frames (~33ms) when there's no CSS transition, regardless of screen size. The `maxAttempts = 60` cap only serves as a safety net for pathological cases.

Fixed in commit dd76df7.

**Root Cause**: The sort comparator `(-Infinity) - (-Infinity)` yields `NaN` when both timestamps are missing, violating ECMA-262's consistent comparison function requirement.

**Fix**: Added equality check before subtraction:
```typescript
const ta = a.updatedAt ?? -Infinity;
const tb = b.updatedAt ?? -Infinity;
if (ta === tb) return 0;
return tb - ta;
```

This ensures the comparator always returns a valid number (`-1`, `0`, or `1`-ish) and is spec-compliant.

**CI Run**: https://github.com/ComposioHQ/agent-orchestrator/actions/runs/22815105769

**Fixed in 41a9fef**

`parsePrFromUrl()` now has a fallback regex to extract PR number from URL ending when the full GitHub URL pattern doesn't match. This preserves the PR number even for non-standard PR URLs.

```typescript
// Full GitHub pattern: extracts owner, repo, and number
github.com/owner/repo/pull/123 → { owner, repo, number: 123 }

// Fallback pattern: extracts number from URL ending
any-url/ending/123 → { owner: '', repo: '', number: 123 }
```

Tests: 406 passed, typecheck clean.
